@Perlover  would you be open to me preparing a small patch on top of your branch? I can add an integration-style regression test using a fake generator that reproduces the early-EOT/single-timestamp case and verifies that the abandoned tail is submitted again and included in the response.

I can also look at keeping recovered segment timestamps monotonic. For the 2.0 s threshold, I’d prefer to verify the behavior against current main and its exact chunk offsets before changing it, since lowering it could increase unnecessary recovery over silence.

I’ll keep the patch focused and send you a commit that can be reviewed and cherry-picked. @ravo42-maker, a rerun on sm_120 afterward would be really helpful.

@Perlover — gentle nudge. @HritickGokul's offer above (2026-09-23) to add a regression test for the early-EOT / single-timestamp case and keep the recovered segments monotonic has been waiting on a reply from you for about five weeks. Are you open to that commit on top of your branch? A yes or a "please send it as a follow-up PR" is all that's needed to unblock it.

For what it's worth, the branch has not gone stale: I test-applied this PR's diff against `main` as of today (`9f0893f`, 2026-10-28) and it applies cleanly. The six commits that have touched `vllm/entrypoints/speech_to_text/base/serving.py` since 2026-08-20 are docstring/lint and unrelated feature work that avoid your hunks. So the only thing holding this up is the review loop, not a rebase.

Since #58029 was consolidated into this PR, it is also the only in-flight fix for the `verbose_json` chunk-cut word loss. Once the test lands, I'll re-run the sm_120 comparison from my earlier comment on the updated branch. If it would help, I'm happy to post the result as a review here so the `ready` label and `/ci run` have something to point at.

@ravo42-maker @HritickGokul — sorry for the long silence, and thank you both: the sm_120 comparison and the analysis in #58029 moved this PR further than anything I did since opening it.

@HritickGokul — yes, please. Open a PR against `Perlover/vllm:fix/whisper-recover-abandoned-audio` and I'll review and merge it there, so it lands in this PR. Both pieces are welcome: the integration-style regression test (the unit tests here cover only the pure helpers), and monotonic recovered segments — the pre-roll exists only so the slice doesn't start mid-word; the timestamps don't need to show it.

What I can add once your commit is in:
- re-run the measurements from the PR description on current `main` with the updated branch — `whisper-large-v3-turbo`, real speech recordings, sm_120 (RTX PRO 4000 Blackwell) — and post the before/after table here. The recordings are private, so numbers only;
- cover the case synthetic clips can't: real silence inside and at the end of a chunk, where the density guard is the only thing between a recovered region and an invented sentence;
- measure the threshold you raised — 1.5 s against 2.0 s on the same recordings, counting both speech recovered and phantoms rejected — so the choice rests on numbers rather than on my original guess. Note that the gap is measured inside the chunk (actual chunk length minus the last decoded timestamp), so chunk offsets don't enter it; whether the 2.32 s stretch from the 16-minute clip is caught on `main` is one of the things this run will show.

@ravo42-maker — your sm_120 re-run on the updated branch plus ours would give the maintainers two independent measurements to point the `ready` label at.

_Written with the help of an AI coding assistant (Claude Code)._

